From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pet0203 <Pet0203@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] spi: mtk-nor: re-initialise the controller in runtime resume

mtk_nor_resume() calls pm_runtime_force_resume() and then mtk_nor_init().
If the controller was already runtime-suspended when the system went to
sleep, pm_runtime_force_resume() does not run the runtime-resume callback,
so the clocks stay gated and mtk_nor_init() writes to a clock-gated block.
On MT8192 (google,spherion) that hangs resume in dpm_resume_end() and the
screen stays black.

Move the controller init into mtk_nor_runtime_resume(), after the clocks
have been enabled, and make system resume only call
pm_runtime_force_resume(). The controller is then re-initialised right
there if it was active at suspend, and on first use otherwise.

The obvious smaller workaround, dropping the pm_runtime_force_resume() call
from system resume, also avoids the hang but is wrong: it leaves runtime PM
disabled after the first suspend, and every later access to the flash
(/dev/mtd0: flashrom, crossystem, firmware updates) fails with
"Failed to power device: -13" until the next reboot.

Tested on spherion: the SPI NOR is back to runtime PM "auto"/"suspended"
after resume and can be read with flashrom -p linux_mtd every time.
---
 drivers/spi/spi-mtk-nor.c | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++------------
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/spi/spi-mtk-nor.c b/drivers/spi/spi-mtk-nor.c
index 63f513917..1eb663188 100644
--- a/drivers/spi/spi-mtk-nor.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-mtk-nor.c
@@ -967,8 +967,19 @@ static int mtk_nor_runtime_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct spi_controller *ctlr = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 	struct mtk_nor *sp = spi_controller_get_devdata(ctlr);
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = mtk_nor_enable_clk(sp);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
 
-	return mtk_nor_enable_clk(sp);
+	/*
+	 * The controller can lose its register state while the clocks are
+	 * gated, so re-initialise it here, where the clocks are running.
+	 */
+	mtk_nor_init(sp);
+
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static int mtk_nor_suspend(struct device *dev)
@@ -978,17 +989,15 @@ static int mtk_nor_suspend(struct device *dev)
 
 static int mtk_nor_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
-	struct spi_controller *ctlr = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
-	struct mtk_nor *sp = spi_controller_get_devdata(ctlr);
-	int ret;
-
-	ret = pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
-	if (ret)
-		return ret;
-
-	mtk_nor_init(sp);
-
-	return 0;
+	/*
+	 * Do not touch the controller registers here. If the controller was
+	 * already runtime-suspended when the system went to sleep,
+	 * pm_runtime_force_resume() leaves its clocks gated, and writing to a
+	 * clock-gated block hangs resume on MT8192 (black screen). The
+	 * controller is re-initialised in mtk_nor_runtime_resume() instead:
+	 * right here if it was active at suspend, otherwise on first use.
+	 */
+	return pm_runtime_force_resume(dev);
 }
 
 static const struct dev_pm_ops mtk_nor_pm_ops = {
